      <h1>⭐ Morning Briefing — October 28, 2026</h1>

      <p class="briefing-subtitle">(Wednesday, October 28, 2026)</p>

      <p class="top-story"><strong>Top Story:</strong> PUC staff says 12 to 20 more Batch Zero appeals are on the way, after Skybox became the fifth developer to take ERCOT to the commission over how its project was classified.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📰 Today's News</h2>

      <h3>1. ⚖️ Five Developers Have Now Appealed ERCOT's Batch Zero Rulings, and PUC Staff Expects Up to 20 More</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Dallas-based Skybox Datacenters and its subsidiary Texas Data Center Campus Power filed a complaint against ERCOT on Monday, Oct. 26 (Docket 60431). The PUC's order identifies the project as Skybox's data center development in Wichita Falls. The megawatt figure is redacted in the public copy; earlier local reporting put the Wichita Falls campus at about 800 MW on 225 acres in the city's business park.</li>
        <li>The complaint says ERCOT's Sept. 3 provisional Batch Zero classification put the project (ERCOT IDs LLI-2233 and BZ-465) in "Studied/Allocated Load" instead of "Base Load." ERCOT ruled the project's already-approved stability study invalid because it left out 19 other large loads that ERCOT identified after approving it. At a Sept. 15 dispute meeting, ERCOT said Oncor hadn't submitted an updated stability study by July 10, as ERCOT requested on June 30. ERCOT denied the dispute Sept. 18, saying the project "remains classified as a studied load."</li>
        <li>Skybox, represented by Baker Botts, asks the commission to reverse ERCOT, restore Base Load status and suspend the classification while the case is pending.</li>
        <li>It isn't alone. PUC staff filed a motion Oct. 23 listing four earlier Batch Zero complaints: Medina County Power NR I (Docket 60356, filed Sept. 30), Rowan Stampede (60396, Oct. 14), Mesquite H2 (60410, Oct. 20) and ACS DC Hicks Field Road (60425, Oct. 22). All four asked for expedited schedules.</li>
        <li>Staff told the commission it "should expect between 12 and 20 additional such complaints in the coming weeks," and warned that, unless they're managed efficiently, "the available resources of ERCOT, Staff and the Commission will be overwhelmed." Staff wants commissioners to set a common process at the Nov. 5 open meeting.</li>
        <li>On Tuesday, Oct. 27, administrative law judges granted the abatement in the four earlier dockets and paused Skybox's case until Nov. 10. ERCOT's response in the Medina County case, due today (Oct. 28), was left in place because staff thinks it will help every case.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> The audit is turning into litigation. By ERCOT's own account, Skybox lost Base Load status partly because its utility, Oncor, didn't refile a study on time, so developers can lose their place over paperwork they don't control. If staff's count is right, the Nov. 5 open meeting will decide how up to two dozen developers get to argue their place in line, and for them that may matter more than the Dec. 10 audit report.</p>

      <h3>2. 📊 UH Poll: 79% of Likely Voters Want a Cap on New Texas Data Centers</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>The University of Houston's Hobby School released the data center part of its Texas Votes 2026 survey Tuesday, Oct. 27. It polled 2,000 likely voters Oct. 10–14, with a margin of error of ±2.19 points.</li>
        <li>79% support limiting how many data centers can be built in Texas (56% strongly), and 99% support making data center companies pay for the grid upgrades they need.</li>
        <li>Asked about a new data center in their own community, 76% expect their electricity bill to rise and 61% expect home values to fall, while 63% expect more local tax revenue.</li>
        <li>69% are extremely or very concerned about electricity prices, and the same share about water supply. Concern about outages is 65%, and about noise and air pollution 53% each.</li>
        <li>The partisan gap is wide. 96% of Democrats expect higher bills, compared with 55% of Republicans, and on air pollution 85% of Democrats are very concerned versus 28% of Republicans. Coverage by ABC13, WFAA (headline/snippet only) and the Houston Chronicle (headline/snippet only) led with the power-bill finding.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> The 99% number matters. It's hard to find an issue that unites every Texas voter, and making data centers pay for their own grid upgrades is close to one. Six days before the election, whoever writes the January bill now has a poll showing both parties' voters expect a new data center to raise their electric bill.</p>

      <h3>3. 🏜️ Hudspeth Commissioners Take Up ServerDome's Road Deal as the CEO Targets December Dirt Work</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Hudspeth County Commissioners Court met Tuesday, Oct. 27, with three ServerDome Global items on the agenda: a presentation on its modular data infrastructure, naming a special advisor for "Good Neighbor Agreements" on large developments, and a road use agreement for the company "pending proper bond filing," KTSM reported. The report didn't say how commissioners voted.</li>
        <li>CEO David Keegan told KTSM the company is waiting on a permit he expects in 30 to 45 days, which would let mass earthmoving start, with site leveling around early December. He didn't say which permit or which agency would issue it.</li>
        <li>Keegan said the Hueco Ranch campus will draw water from a private aquifer on site instead of regional supplies. He said it will use 500-foot domes and take three to four years to bring a gigawatt online. Early hiring would include janitorial, catering and facility jobs, plus a technician training program.</li>
        <li>The project's cost is still reported inconsistently: KTSM's body text says $15 million, while its headline and other outlets say $15 billion.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> A road use agreement is usually where a county first gets real leverage. Commissioners can ask for a bond, so they don't end up paying to fix roads that heavy trucks wear out. Residents should ask what that bond covers and which permit Keegan is waiting on, because a 1-GW gas plant needs a TCEQ air permit, the kind of permit Abbott's pause covers.</p>

      <h3>4. 🏛️ More Texas Cities Take Back the Vote on Data Centers</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>College Station City Council unanimously voted Oct. 22 to redefine data centers so they can go only in business parks and light-to-heavy industrial districts, and only with a conditional use permit, Community Impact reported Tuesday. City Manager Bryan Woods acknowledged the city is "constrained by what the state and other rules say."</li>
        <li>In San Antonio, Mayor Gina Ortiz Jones visited a Bexar County data center about 650 feet from a playground and pool on Monday, Oct. 26, ahead of Thursday's council vote, the San Antonio Observer reported. That site is outside city limits.</li>
        <li>Jones's "Option 2+" would require a council vote on each new data center, plus 45 days' pre-filing notice, two neighborhood meetings, air-quality monitoring and twice the green space removed during construction. Councilmembers McKee-Rodriguez, Mungia, Castillo, Galvan and Meza Gonzalez back case-by-case review, while Whyte and Spears warn it could drive away investment.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="signal"><strong>Today's Signal:</strong> Cities can't stop a data center outright, so they're using conditional permits and council votes to get a say. The site Jones toured makes the case against that approach, though: it's in the county, outside city limits, where none of these ordinances apply.</p>

      <p>Quiet on TCEQ: the Executive Director's air-permit agenda lists only a Harrison County renewal, and the Nov. 4 commission agenda has no data center or gas-generation items, though it does include wastewater matters in Wise, Hood, Denton and Tarrant counties. The Texas Tribune has published nothing new on data centers since Oct. 21, ERCOT still has no news release since Sept. 21, and nothing has been reported yet from Denton's second moratorium hearing Tuesday night.</p>

      <hr class="section-divider" />

      <h2>📍 Today's Watch List</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>ERCOT's response in the Medina County Power docket (60356), due today, which staff says will shape every Batch Zero complaint.</li>
        <li>PUC open meeting Nov. 5, where staff will ask commissioners to set a process for Batch Zero appeals. Skybox's case is paused until Nov. 10, Skybox must serve the Office of Public Utility Counsel by Nov. 3, and ERCOT must notify qualified scheduling entities and its committees by Nov. 9.</li>
        <li>How many of the 12 to 20 complaints staff expects actually get filed, and which utilities' missed or late studies they cite.</li>
        <li>San Antonio City Council's data center vote Thursday, Oct. 29, at 9 a.m. (Agenda Item 5).</li>
        <li>Whether Hudspeth commissioners approved ServerDome's road use agreement, what the bond covers, and which permit Keegan expects in 30–45 days. He's also scheduled to take questions at the county's Halloween event Oct. 31.</li>
        <li>Denton's moratorium: coverage of Tuesday's second hearing, with the earliest adoption vote Dec. 1.</li>
        <li>ERCOT Technical Advisory Committee meets today. ERCOT's SB 6 large-load curtailment workshop is Nov. 6.</li>
        <li>Early voting ends Oct. 30, and Election Day is Nov. 3.</li>
        <li>Texas Pacific Land reports Nov. 4, and Vistra and NRG report in early November.</li>
        <li>PUC open meeting Nov. 19, the same day as ERCOT's Large Load Working Group. Batch/CTP workshops are Nov. 17 and 19, and Docket 60368 interventions are due Nov. 19.</li>
        <li>The Keller residents' standing hearing over Westlake's Circle T vote, still unreported.</li>
        <li>ERCOT's Planning Working Group meets Nov. 9.</li>
        <li>ERCOT audit reports are due Dec. 10, and the PUC meets Dec. 17.</li>
      </ul>

